{"title":"2010 CEMC Cayley Problems/Problem 10","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Pascal_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2010 CEMC Pascal Problems (page does not exist)\">2010 CEMC Pascal #13</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Cayley_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2010 CEMC Cayley Problems (page does not exist)\">2010 CEMC Cayley #10</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(answer_choices)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (answer choices)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Differences_in_Pascal_Contest\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Differences in Pascal Contest</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e8924412a12becf7905729885a46e3e351ec5a86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> students at Pascal H.S., where the ratio of boys to girls is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/e/80e47d95347d6e72d1b3e8a4aea3dd20082f3cc3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3:2$\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" />. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/a/caa9bc3e1ca566d171ec6b80e39ee4cc913b5aa1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$600$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> students at Fermat C.I., where the ratio of boys to girls is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/b/37bac8710233ca570649f0526e28b1a1ebb69d1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2:3$\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" />. When considering all the students from both schools, what is the ratio of boys to girls?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/9/8d972968ef04c21e827b7e9680217392dcd159d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{ (A) }\\ 2:3 \\qquad\\text{ (B) }\\ 12:13 \\qquad\\text{ (C) }\\ 1:1 \\qquad\\text{ (D) }\\ 6:5 \\qquad\\text{ (E) }\\ 3:2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"527\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Because of the ratios given for Pascal H.S., we can let the number of boys at Pascal H.S. be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/3/89385605cebfe24cd5d7a9402a2908b64a012fa4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"16\" />, the number of girls at Pascal H.S. be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/c/26cab3f0704de3232f601b03d02a45d46103c172.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" />. Using the ratios given for Fermat C.I., we can also let the number of boys at that school be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/c/2ec701493fa72dda5d6747fe14f30e7b1c2fb6c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/e/70e634348f8df9eaeac4ced35db64d08a906532e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>Since there's <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e8924412a12becf7905729885a46e3e351ec5a86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> students at Pascal H.S., we have:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/1/6b11900212017a32418e011ed328035344533029.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3y + 2y = 5y = 400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"156\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/a/65a1d13e96060a46d976205b8ffb8e6b4eab98f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = \\frac{400}{5} = 80$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"109\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Since there's <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/a/caa9bc3e1ca566d171ec6b80e39ee4cc913b5aa1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$600$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" /> students at Fermat C.I., we have:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/8/8a803955a6654e897f5fa15c4df36377a5d89917.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2x + 3x = 5x = 600$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"158\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/e/14ed6191dbdec8ad5bb5fb1b1e55f36ae2b0f493.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{600}{5} = 120$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"119\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>The number of boys at Pascal H.S. is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/1/e61c1e3c71b717ef82686e551d97428b3afa89c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 \\times 80 = 240$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"103\" height=\"15\" />. The number of boys at the other school is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/5/f253ba4edf48f5c02383201da6aa2ec8e6dbdcd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 120 = 240$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"112\" height=\"15\" />. Adding these together, we find that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/8/898a399c4ff26b0a1818982ac9482d136016280c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$240 + 240 = 480$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"130\" height=\"15\" /> boys in total.\n</p><p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/2/b62d4f1cb35ba486c94dd178bb3e47b973a7b136.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$400 + 600 = 1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"139\" height=\"15\" /> students in total, so there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/7/3576917a7a5c9731efd8fcb18676be15a92ddeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000 - 480 = 520$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"139\" height=\"15\" /> girls.\n</p><p>The ratio of boys to girls is then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/4/144a3e6f19e5aee834263abec237786ba4ab66ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$480:520$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"72\" height=\"14\" />. Simplifying the ratio, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/1/f514cc0aa38ce682c14b28c637db7d8d09a83cbf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(B) } 12:13}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"99\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28answer_choices.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(answer_choices)\">Solution 2 (answer choices)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (answer choices)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Without simplifying, we can notice that there are more girls than boys in total because the ratio is flipped at the second school, which has more students. This means that the ratio must be less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, eliminating all answer choices except for A and B.\n</p><p>Next, we notice that the combined ratio must be more than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/b/37bac8710233ca570649f0526e28b1a1ebb69d1e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2:3$\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" /> but less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/e/80e47d95347d6e72d1b3e8a4aea3dd20082f3cc3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3:2$\" width=\"32\" height=\"12\" /> because we are combining the students from the two schools. This eliminates answer choices A and E.\n</p><p>All of the other answer answer choices have been eliminated, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/1/f514cc0aa38ce682c14b28c637db7d8d09a83cbf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed {\\textbf {(B) } 12:13}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"99\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Differences_in_Pascal_Contest\">Differences in Pascal Contest</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Cayley_Problems/Problem_10&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Differences in Pascal Contest\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The only difference in the Pascal Contest version of the problem is that the school is \"Cayley H.S.,\" rather than \"Pascal H.S.\"\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Pascal\" title=\"2010 CEMC Pascal\">2010 CEMC Pascal</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Pascal_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2010 CEMC Pascal Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Pascal_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2010 CEMC Pascal Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_12&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2010 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 12 (page does not exist)\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_14&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2010 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 14 (page does not exist)\">Problem 14</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2010 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2010 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2010 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2010 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2010_CEMC_Pascal_Problems/Problem_5&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2010 CEMC Pascal Problems/Problem 5 (page does not exist)\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a